---
YUME carries TCP, UDP and IP packets from a client to a server over a
connection that looks like a browser visiting an ordinary HTTPS site. The
client is `yume` and the server is `yumed`. Both speak YTP/1, the YUME
Transport Protocol.

The product version is `0.3.0-dev1`. There is no stable release yet, and Linux
x86-64 is the first target.

- Website: <https://yume.fixcraft.jp>
- Source: <https://github.com/FixCraft-Inc/yume>
- Issues: <https://github.com/FixCraft-Inc/yume/issues>

## What it does

Applications reach `yume` through a local SOCKS5 listener (TCP CONNECT and UDP
ASSOCIATE), or the whole Linux machine routes through a managed TUN device.
`yume` carries every connection over one session to `yumed`, which opens the
destination sockets allowed by its policy.

On the wire the session is TLS 1.3 with a pinned Chrome ClientHello, HTTP/2
and WebSocket framing. A connection without a valid admission proof gets the
server's cover website. Clients authenticate with composite Ed25519 and
ML-DSA-87 identities. Session keys combine ML-KEM-1024, X25519, a pre-shared
key and the TLS exporter, and every record uses a one-use AES-256-GCM key.

`yumed` sees which client connected and which destinations it opens. Use TLS
inside the tunnel when content must stay private from the server operator.
[Implementation status](docs/IMPLEMENTATION_STATUS.md) lists what is tested
and what still blocks a release.

## Build

@code bash
git clone https://github.com/FixCraft-Inc/yume.git
cd yume
./ezbuild.sh
@end

This produces `build/bin/yume` and `build/bin/yumed` and prepares the pinned,
patched OpenSSL that the Chrome TLS profile needs. Direct CMake builds must use
the same OpenSSL installation. See [the contributor guide](CONTRIBUTING.md).

## Set up a tunnel

`yume-setup` writes a server and client kit: configuration, credentials and a
cover site.

@code bash
sudo cmake --install build
yume-setup init \
  --output ~/yume-kit \
  --host yume.example.com \
  --port 443 \
  --client-name laptop
@end

The [quick start](docs/QUICKSTART.md) continues from here. The kit contains
private keys. Move client files over a trusted channel and keep the operator
CA key off the server.

## Programs

@table
| Program | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `yume` | Client: SOCKS5 TCP/UDP listener and Linux managed TUN |
| `yumed` | Server: TLS/HTTP/2 front door, cover site, authentication, policy and exit |
| `yume-setup` | Creates server and client kits, adds and removes clients |
| `yume-doctor` | Checks configuration and credential files |
| `libyume` | Optional C library for embedding the client or server in another program |
@end

The desktop GUI and some older features (relay, federation, local forwards,
the JSON control API) still live in the transport-v2 reference build and are
being moved to YTP/1. That build is off by default and not installed.

## Documentation

The [documentation map](docs/README.md) lists every page. Most readers need:

- [YUME explained](docs/EXPLAINED.md) for how a connection works and what
  each party can see
- [quick start](docs/QUICKSTART.md) and [operations](docs/OPERATIONS.md) to run
  a server and clients
- [threat model](docs/THREAT_MODEL.md) and [stealth](docs/STEALTH.md) for the
  security claims and their limits
- [architecture](docs/ARCHITECTURE.md), [C ABI](docs/ABI.md) and
  [YTP/1](docs/protocol/YTP_1.md) to embed or change YUME

